# The capture writes six frames by default; two of them are committed as
# documentation. README.md says which two and why.
#
# The other four must not be committed, and that cannot be left to whoever runs
# the capture next. The fourth working agreement is explicit about it: "if a
# generated file must **not** be committed, that has to be arranged by
# construction — an ignore rule — and not by remembering."
#
# Without this file, a plain `tools/capture-match-frames.ps1` leaves four
# untracked PNGs sitting in the tree, and the build gate's last step — the one
# that fails a push whose run rewrote the repository — goes red for a reason
# that has nothing to do with the push.
#
# The two negations are not load-bearing today, because an ignore rule cannot
# govern a file git already tracks. They are here so that re-adding either
# frame after a deliberate deletion works without anybody having to remember
# this file exists.
*.png
!match-tick-0347.png
!match-tick-2700.png
